Continuity — Introduction

Doctors see patients for minutes. Continuity remembers the days in between.

The Problem

Anyone managing an ongoing condition — arthritis, high blood pressure, diabetes — has heard the same instruction from their doctor: "keep track of your symptoms between visits." 
In practice, almost no one does this reliably, and elderly patients are hit hardest by it. Symptoms come and go across many small, forgettable moments — a stiff knee on Tuesday,
a dizzy spell Thursday morning, trouble sleeping over the weekend — and by the time the next appointment arrives, weeks later, most of that detail has simply faded.
What the patient remembers is usually a vague summary: "my knee was hurting." The specifics — how often, how severe, whether it was getting worse, what else came with it — are gone.

The result is that the doctor spends the first several minutes of a short appointment not treating the patient, but trying to reconstruct a fuzzy timeline from an unreliable memory.
This isn't a hypothetical inconvenience — it's a routine, expensive gap in ongoing care, and it's especially unfair to elderly patients, 
for whom typing into a symptom-tracking app is itself a barrier. The tools that exist to solve this problem require exactly the kind of daily,
manual effort this population is least equipped to sustain.

